  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provision of WASH and FSL emergency services to meet the needs of returnees and refugees from Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">UN June 2024 report estimates that 707,000 people have moved to South Sudan through various border points - 80% are South Sudanese returnees and 20% refugees. South Sudan struggles to provide basic services such as food, water, sanitation, healthcare, and protection to these vulnerable people. UNHCR estimates the displaced population staying in transit camps in Renk County is 22% children, 49% men and boys, 51% women and girls, and 15% people with disabilities. Refugees and returnees are expected to stay in transit camps for one week and then move to more permanent locations. According to UNHCR, 40,000 newly displaced arrivals are expected in July 2024. Services in the transit centre and surrounding host communities are poor and identified gaps include WASH (latrines, SWM, NFIs); food, emergency shelter/housing, protection and GBV support. 
Tr&#xf3;caire, through its partner ADA is already implementing an emergency small scale project through internal micro-funding which includes water trucking in Renk Transit Centre (TC) alongside other agencies but given the increasing numbers of returnees and refugees from Sudan and the escalating needs, the support of Irish Aid is requested to allow Tr&#xf3;caire to strengthen the response to this new acute crisis through WASH and FSL programming.</narrative>
  </description>
